    "Sudden cardiac death (SCD) occurs across various ages, with incidence skewed between 31 and 60 years, showing a significant male predominance. The main causes of SCD include ischemic heart disease and cardiomyopathies. Genetic testing is crucial, as it helps identify potential hereditary causes of unexpected deaths. Understanding these genetic components can aid in family screening and targeted management.": {
      "1. Comprehensive cardiac screening can help detect potential hereditary cardiac issues. This includes using 12-lead ECGs and other monitoring techniques to potentially identify a heritable cardiac cause of death.": {
        "2. Genetic testing should be based on the initial evaluation results, with particular reference to known conditions like long QT syndrome, catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, Brugada syndrome, and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.": {
          "3. Family members of SCD victims should undergo risk profiling and genetic testing to identify disease-causing mutations for better targeted family screening.\u201d": {
            "4. Regarding terminal care, ICD deactivation should be part of advance care planning discussions to align treatment with patient preferences.": {
              "5. Families are advised to discuss ICD deactivation in advance, as decisions are often needed urgently without prior discussion.": "genes"
            }
          }
        }
      }
